---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Corrections policy: MemJudge"
  description="How to report a wrong or unfair result, and what MemJudge does about it."
>
  <article class="legal">
    <h1>Corrections policy</h1>
    <p class="muted">Last updated 19 October 2026</p>
    <p>
      MemJudge aims to be fair to every system it tests. If you think a result is wrong, unfair or
      should not be published, tell us.
    </p>

    <h2>How to report</h2>
    <p>
      Email <a href="mailto:admin@memjudge.org">admin@memjudge.org</a> or open an issue at{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/memjudge/memjudge/issues">github.com/memjudge/memjudge</a>. Say
      which result (its link), what is wrong and what you would like done. Anyone can report;
      vendors of a tested system get priority. We aim to reply within 3 business days.
    </p>

    <h2>What we can do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Correct</strong>, when the setup was wrong: for example a connector that did not
        follow the vendor's documented configuration. We fix it and re-run, or invite the vendor to.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Label</strong>, when a result stands but has a known limit: for example "self-hosted
        open-source version" or "the service cannot take conversation dates".
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Remove</strong>, when publishing breaks a law, a court order or the terms that bind
        the person who ran it. While a report is decided we may hide the disputed result.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Right of reply and records</h2>
    <p>
      A vendor can ask for its response to be linked next to its results. Every correction or
      removal is recorded in the public{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/memjudge/results/blob/main/CORRECTIONS.md">corrections log</a>{" "}
      with its date and reason. Results are signed, so a corrected result is published as a new,
      labelled result rather than by editing the original. A low score alone is not a reason to
      remove a result.
    </p>

    <h2>How we publish</h2>
    <p>
      We publish first and tell the vendor the same day, with links to the result and the connector
      code; we do not wait for permission. Every run of a connector stays listed on its run page, so
      a re-run after a fix never hides the earlier result. The fairness rules we follow are in{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/memjudge/memjudge/blob/main/docs/publishing-results.md">
        publishing results
      </a>
      .
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
